    notes: `<b>DYNAMIC PSYCHOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT</b><br><br><b>1. Definition</b><br><br>Dynamic Psychological Assessment is an interactive approach that evaluates a person's abilities, provides an intervention or help, and evaluates the person again to see if improvement occurs.<br><br><b>2. Explanation</b><br><br>Dynamic Psychological Assessment focuses not only on what a person can do now, but also on how the person can improve with help or guidance.<br><br>It follows three steps:<br><ol><li><b>Evaluation</b> – Assess the person's current abilities.</li><li><b>Intervention</b> – Provide help, teaching, or guidance.</li><li><b>Evaluation</b> – Assess the person again to check for improvement.</li></ol>This process is also called the <b>"sandwich method."</b><br><br><b>3. Example</b><br><br>A psychologist gives a child a problem-solving task and observes that the child has difficulty answering it. The psychologist teaches the child a strategy and then gives another similar task to see whether the child performs better.<br><br>This is Dynamic Psychological Assessment because the psychologist evaluates the child, provides help, and evaluates the child again.<br><br><b>4. What to Remember for CFE</b><br><ul><li>It is an interactive approach.</li><li>It follows evaluation ➔ intervention ➔ evaluation.</li><li>It checks how a person responds to help or instruction.</li><li>It is also known as the sandwich method.</li></ul><b>5. Situational Application</b><br><br><b>Scenario:</b> A psychologist assesses a student's reading ability, teaches the student a new reading strategy, and then assesses the student's reading again.<br><br><b>Answer:</b> Dynamic Psychological Assessment.<br><br><b>Why?</b> The psychologist evaluates the student before and after providing an intervention.<br><br><b>6. CFE Tip: How to Recognize It</b><br><br><b>Clue words:</b> Evaluation, intervention, improvement, and sandwich method.<br><br><b>Easy way to remember:</b> Test ➔ Teach ➔ Test again.`
