    correctAnswer: "Cut Score",
    notes: `<b>CUT SCORE</b><br><br><b>1. Definition</b><br><br>A Cut Score is a reference point or score used to divide test results into two or more classifications.<br><br><b>2. Explanation</b><br><br>A cut score helps determine how a person's test result should be classified, such as pass or fail, or whether the person meets a required level of ability.<br><br>There are different types of cut scores:<br><ul><li><b>Relative Cut Score</b> – Based on comparisons with other test takers or norms.</li><li><b>Fixed Cut Score</b> – Based on a set minimum level of proficiency or ability.</li><li><b>Multiple Cut Scores</b> – Uses two or more cut scores for one predictor.</li></ul><b>3. Example</b><br><br>A school sets a passing score of 75 on an examination.<br><ul><li>Score of 75 or higher = Pass</li><li>Score below 75 = Fail</li></ul>The score of 75 is the cut score because it separates the two classifications.<br><br><b>4. What to Remember for CFE</b><br><ul><li>A cut score is a reference point used to classify test results.</li><li>It can help determine whether someone passes or meets a required standard.</li><li>A relative cut score is based on norms or comparisons.</li><li>A fixed cut score is based on a required minimum level of proficiency.</li></ul><b>5. Situational Application</b><br><br><b>Situation:</b> A company requires applicants to obtain a minimum score of 80 on an aptitude test to qualify for the next stage of hiring.<br><br><b>Answer:</b> Fixed Cut Score<br><br><b>Why?</b> The company uses a predetermined minimum score to decide who qualifies.<br><br><b>6. CFE Tip: How to Recognize It</b><br><br><b>Clue words:</b> passing score, minimum score, reference point, classification, pass or fail.<br><br><b>Easy way to remember:</b><br><br><b>Cut Score = The dividing line between classifications.</b>`
